ne we  we incorporate uh  perhaps two to three uh reference genes  in each of our multiplexes in this case  we had already used gap dh and b2m  for validation of the real-time targets  and also in the microarray so it made  sense to incorporate these  targets in the multiplex  the next step is that these  the  sequences which are  associated with the session numbers are  then  uploaded into the genome lab system  software system  and we use the express profiler  component of this to actually design  our gene-specific  primers for each of the targets  we then take each of those primers and  we will then run them through some sort  of search engines such as blast  just to  make sure that they don't have any off  targets or non-target homology it's  going to cause problems in the asses  and we also gather information on the  structural information of each of the  targets such as intron exon boundaries  for example and we try to ensure we're  possible that our primary assets will  amplify of course across the intro and  x1 boundaries  when we're happy with the primary  selections  we then begin  to conduct  the first step of optimizing the rca  now this requires of course  choosing your template rna  in our case we've been using  colon  mucosa  and this slide just simply shows a  typical colon mucosal sample this one  actually is  an ex-evil cultured colon explant but  from this you can clearly see from the  grade  gradated scale here  that these are very small tissue samples  and we typically use around 20 to 13  milligrams of tissue for rna extraction  following the extraction we then assess  quality using both agilent analysis and  also nanodrop spectrophotometry  now for those of you unfamiliar with the  genome system gexp asses um i will just  take uh  go through the next couple of slides  just the principles behind the assays  now of course when we design a new  multiplex the first step is to actually  then conduct a single plex of each of  our individual chain sp